          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cell: The Building Block of Life"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "17"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.089,
            0.499,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ribosomes — The protein factories"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.111,
            0.579,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These are tiny structures may be present either freely in the cytoplasm or attached to the endoplasmic reticulum. Ribosomes are the sites of protein synthesis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.189,
            0.429,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER) — Manufacturing factory"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.231,
            0.579,
            0.367
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER) is a large organelle that spreads like a network within the cytoplasm of the cell. The ER is continuous with the outer membrane of nuclear envelop. The ER plays a key role in the synthesis and transport of proteins, fats (lipids), and some hormones in some of the specialised cell. The structure of the ER in a cell varies depending on its function. There are two types of ER:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.369,
            0.579,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER):",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " It looks rough under an electron microscope because it has ribosomes attached to its surface, and is mainly involved in protein synthesis and protein secretion (for example, in gland cells, such as pancreatic cells)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.474,
            0.579,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER):",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " It does not have ribosomes on its surface, and therefore, looks smooth. It is involved in the synthesis, and storage of fats and hormones (Fig. 2.13)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.551,
            0.578,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Golgi apparatus — The packaging and shipping centres"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.597,
            0.58,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you carefully look at the diagram of a cell (Fig. 2.10b and c), you will notice stacks of flattened, sac-like structures. Together, these stacks form the Golgi apparatus (Fig. 2.13). It is functionally linked to the ER, the cell membrane and the other cell organelles. The Golgi apparatus acts like the cell’s post office. It modifies, sorts, and packages proteins and/or lipids into vesicles for transport, secretion, or lysosome formation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.761,
            0.49,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Lysosomes — The clean-up system"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.783,
            0.579,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cells produce waste materials and damaged, worn-out organelles during their activities. How does the cell prevent these wastes from accumulating inside it?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.854,
            0.579,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Lysosomes",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are single membrane-bound sacs filled with enzymes, which can break down unwanted proteins, carbohydrates, fats, and even"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.592,
            0.088,
            0.961,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
