        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.768,
            0.541,
            0.925,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Golgi apparatus",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " was first observed in 1898 by an Italian scientist, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Camillo Golgi",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", in the nerve cells of a barn owl. Using special"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.662,
            0.922,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "staining techniques, he observed a thread-like network. Early microscopes could not resolve it clearly, therefore, many doubted its existence. However, electron microscope observations confirmed it decades later."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.65,
            0.772,
            0.919,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When the structure was clearly seen and confirmed, it was named the ‘Golgi apparatus’ in his honour."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
